  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 329 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThrough Thursday: Additional dangerous heat stress is the primary\nconcern as we go through the remainder of the afternoon and then\nagain Thursday, and the heat advisory has been extended in time\nfor most of the area accordingly. A weak frontal boundary that is\nsupporting isolated storms over mainly northern portions of the \noutlook area today will support renewed storm development Thursday\nafternoon further south, and this will help to limit the heat \njust a bit compared today. Storms are expected to remain below \nsevere limits for the most part. /EC/\n\nThe long-term forecast from the end of the week into early next\nweek remains on track:\n\nIncreased chances for convective activity and cloud coverage will\ntake hold due to the surface confluence from the aforementioned \nfront, which could provide a few degree dip, thus mitigating heat \nthreat concerns. With upper flow becoming more northerly and \nnorthwesterly during this time frame, we will see the potential \nfor one or two convective complexes moving into the area or \ndeveloping over the area. This will result in higher rain chances\nacross the area Friday heading into the weekend. This system will\nalso lose momentum and stall over the area which will bring \nhigher than average diurnal rain chances across the area to finish\nout the period. Going into early next week, we'll see rain chances\ndimninish and heat potentially rebuild some, but not to the extent\nthat we are currently observiing. /OAJ/EC/",
